<p>Forward, up, and right vectors are initially created using world transfo=
rm data; however since the up and right vectors are created using the forwa=
rd vector, the roll is not accounted for as a 3 dimensional vector has no c=
oncept of roll. To get around this a new right vector is created by rotatin=
g it using sin and cos, then the up vector can be recalculated by crossing =
the right and forward vectors since we know all the vectors should be perpe=
ndicular.</p>
<img src=3D"https://www.jamiekuppens.com/images/content/camera-vectors-visu=
alized.webp" alt=3D"A 3D graphic showing that +Y is up, +Z is forward, and =
+X is right." loading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" fetchpriority=3D"auto" wi=
dth=3D"1920" height=3D"1080" class=3D"bg-gray-100">
<p>A target vector is also used to generate the view matrix which is a poin=
t 1 unit in front of the camera. This position can be calculated by adding =
the forward vector to the world position (all values in forward sum up to 1=
). With that we have the information required to generate the view matrix w=
ith <code>lookAt</code>. The projection matrix is created in the constructo=
r and the view-projection matrix is generated by multiplying the view and p=
rojection matrices. The view-projection matrix is cached so it doesn=E2=80=
=99t need to be recalculated every frame.</p>
<p>In order to make objects appear from the camera=E2=80=99s point of view,=
 their world transform needs to be multiplied with the camera=E2=80=99s vie=
w-projection matrix before drawing. This can be cached as well but is not d=
one in this example to keep things simple.</p>
