<p>Now our final matrices are pushed to the vertex shader and some spinning=
 cubes appear on-screen from the camera=E2=80=99s perspective.</p>
<img src=3D"https://www.jamiekuppens.com/images/content/spinning-cubes.webp=
" alt=3D"A screenshot of a program rendering two dirt cubes on a blue backg=
round." loading=3D"lazy" decoding=3D"async" fetchpriority=3D"auto" width=3D=
"1920" height=3D"1136">
<h2 id=3D"whats-left">What=E2=80=99s Left?</h2>
<p>This implementation does not handle reparenting gracefully like most sce=
ne graphs do as local coordinates are not changed when the parent transform=
 changes. Rendering is also managed outside the walker and is in the main l=
oop to keep the code simple; in a production project this would be managed =
elsewhere. There is also much potential for optimization when it comes to c=
aching values and generating the view matrix.</p>
<p>Although very bare-bones, that=E2=80=99s a working scenegraph in action!=
 Feel free to play around with the code or repurpose it as it=E2=80=99s lic=
ensed under the liberal CC0 license. You can get it on <a href=3D"https://g=
ithub.com/amdrel/scenegraph-demo">GitHub</a>.</p>
